{"id":"refine_20260929133021288","summary":"Updated the global task-cf510dbb memory with the demo-supported rectangle-restoration rule and a clear caution that the current test remains unsolved.","rationale":"Eight demo pairs support restoring solid axis-aligned monochrome rectangles, including background-valued holes, while clearing isolated clutter. The 27×24 test failed all eight submissions; guesses based on single adjacent pixels did not establish rectangle boundaries.","expectedOutcome":"Future solves should distinguish strong bounding-box evidence from adjacency, assess background-valued cells in context, and validate every proposed rectangle and outside cell before submission without treating this test's guesses as correct.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"task_cf510dbb_noisy_rectangular_patch_reconstruction","title":"Continual-ARC task-cf510dbb: restore noisy rectangular patches; validate bounds conservatively","content":"For Continual-ARC task-cf510dbb, restore intended solid, axis-aligned monochrome rectangular patches and reset isolated or non-rectangular clutter to the instance's background color. Eight demo pairs support restoring patch cells corrupted to arbitrary colors or to the background, including background-valued holes when they lie inside a rectangle supported by its other cells. Noise colors can also occur in genuine patches, so color alone does not identify clutter. Infer each rectangle's bounds from the full spatial pattern; do not extend a rectangle merely because a single adjacent noise pixel could fit an expanded box. Enumerate observed cells and candidate bounds carefully, then check every row and column of each proposed box, its interior, and cells outside all boxes. Preserve separate patches even when they share a color. The 27×24 test instance in the refinement trajectory remained unsolved after eight submissions; its guessed coordinates and edge extensions are not validated and must not be treated as a solut