{"id":"refine_20260929134318545","summary":"Created a global Continual-ARC memory for task-864067f3 with the validated panel-counting rule and solved test output.","rationale":"Three demonstrations and a correctly solved fresh input support the rule; the failed attempts also show the importance of counting exactly two markers and using the current panel background color.","expectedOutcome":"Future instances can partition the grid at uniform dividers, count markers within each panel, and encode the panel grid accurately; validate divider locations, panel bounds, marker counts, and output dimensions.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"task_864067f3_panel_marker_count","title":"Continual-ARC task-864067f3: encode panels with exactly two markers","content":"For Continual-ARC task-864067f3, full-width uniform divider rows and full-height divider columns partition the input into rectangular panels. Count the non-background, non-divider marker cells in each panel. Produce a compressed grid with one output cell per panel, in panel row-major order: write color 1 if and only if that panel contains exactly two markers; otherwise write the panel's original background color (the most common fill color, not a hard-coded demo color). Panels with zero or one marker map to the background. Carefully verify divider positions, panel bounds, and every marker count before encoding. Validated on three demonstrations and a solved fresh input; the fresh input had a 4×3 panel layout and output [[3,1,3],[3,1,3],[1,3,3],[3,3,3]]. Earlier failed guesses resulted from treating any multiple markers as qualifying, hard-coding the demo background, and miscounting markers.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"This task recurs across instances, and the rule is supported by multiple demonstrations and a correctly solved fresh input.","after":{"id":"task_864067f3_panel_marker_count","kind":"memory","title":"Continual-ARC task-864067f3: encode panels with exactly two markers",